  "answers": [
    {
      "question": "What has the win rate been?",
      "answer": "55.56% of 18 cycles on committed capital, against 77.78% for four equal monthly buys. Counting only the 15 cycles where it invested at all, 66.67%. Adding a December catch-up for unfilled tranches lifts it to 66.67%."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is it worse than buying four equal monthly tranches?",
      "answer": "Not established. The paired difference is -2.227 percentage points against a resolution bound of 4.59 on 18 cycles, so the sample cannot separate them on return. It is ahead in 8 cycles and behind in 10. The case against it is structural, not statistical."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the structural problem?",
      "answer": "It does not stage the entry. Fills are decided by where the August close sits inside the January-August range: with August in the lower half all five tranches filled in 8 of 8 cycles (mean 5.0 of 5), and 75.0% of those fills were marketable at the open — a market buy, not a retracement buy. With August in the upper half the mean is 1.9 of 5. So it commits everything at once when gold is weak into September and holds back when gold is strong, which is backwards for a seasonal long."
    },
    {
      "question": "What does the shortfall come from?",
      "answer": "Cash, not bad prices. 3 of 18 cycles never filled a single tranche and sat in cash through the whole window. The worst of these is 2025, where the baseline returned 26.685% — the largest single-cycle gain in the sample — and the Fibonacci rule returned nothing."
    }
  ],
